abstract: >
  The ambidextrous moving sofa problem asks for the largest shape that can be
  manoeuvred around a right-angled corner of a unit-width hallway turning both
  ways. Romik's candidate Sigma has area A_R* = 1.6449552184254408...; its
  optimality is open. (Baek, arXiv:2411.19826, settled the one-corner problem,
  proving Gerver's sofa optimal; his hallway has a single corner and his argument
  does not address the ambidextrous variant.)

  This work develops the cap/niche decomposition Sigma = C_2 minus two congruent
  niches, in which the corner path is AFFINE in the cap's support function, so the
  two arms and the reach are convex-linear. In those coordinates the niche area has
  an exact closed form, the cap area is an exact quadratic form, and the functional
  Q = |C_2| - 2V satisfies Q(Sigma) = A_R* structurally, with no integral evaluated.
  Its first variation vanishes identically in Romik's constants, so his ODE families
  ARE the Euler-Lagrange equations of Q.

  The load-bearing hypothesis is (RC): the cap is nowhere flatter than a circle of
  the corridor's width, i.e. the absolutely continuous part of H + H'' is at most 1.
  (RC) is linear in the support function, hence convex; it is classical, being the
  dual of Blaschke's rolling theorem, so what is new is its use. (RC) implies all
  three injectivity conditions, via a forced harmonic oscillator whose kernel is
  non-negative because the rotation angle is pi/2.

  Main theorem: on the convex domain D cut out by (RC), a separation inequality and
  Sigma's own sign pattern, every ambidextrous sofa T with cap data H = H_Sigma + eta
  satisfies |T| <= A_R* - (73/100) ||eta||^2 in L^2(0,pi), with Sigma the unique member
  attaining A_R*. The constant is sharp to within 0.1 percent: setting p(t) = eta(t) and
  q(t) = eta(t + pi/2) turns the second variation into a single 2x2 Sturm-Liouville
  system on [0,pi/2], whose first eigenvalue c* = 0.7309566... IS the best constant,
  computed as a product of three 4x4 matrix exponentials. On the region where the
  obstruction lives, the coupling between the two halves is a total derivative, so it
  contributes only a boundary value and no pointwise splitting is needed. Every step of
  the certificate is carried out in arb ball arithmetic, with beta enclosed from
  u^3 + 3u = 2 by exact rational bisection; nothing in it is floating point.

  Limits are recorded rather than smoothed over. D is a C^2-neighbourhood of Sigma
  (its radius along sin(2k theta) scales like k^-2); (RC) is sharp; the separation
  inequality is independent of (RC) and cannot be removed; and V is the exact flux of
  the advancing wedge boundary, so V >= |N| by Reynolds' transport theorem and Q
  bounds nothing where the sweep fails to be injective. In particular the construction
  does not recover Gerver's sofa, whose cap violates (RC). Optimality of Sigma remains
  open. Lean 4: 880 theorems, no sorry. The 114-theorem core (Basic.lean) is
  standalone -- it has no imports at all, carries trigonometric quantities as formal
  symbols under Pythagorean side conditions, and works over Int so the identities stay
  decidable. The remaining 766 theorems use Mathlib, and include the instantiation of
  those formal symbols at actual sines and cosines.

  An earlier line of work in this repository, on local maximality via second variation
  in H^2, is WITHDRAWN; see paper/PROGRAM.md.

keywords:
  - moving sofa problem
  - Gerver's sofa
  - ambidextrous sofa
  - Romik
